rced words exceed budget? Then no solution? The problem might assume it's always possible. But we need to handle if forced words already exceed budget, then we cannot include all forced words. Could return empty or error? The task doesn't specify, but likely forced words are within budget. We'll assume budget >= sum of forced words' token counts.\n\nWe implement DP for subset sum on remaining words. Since number of words may be up to maybe a few hundred? But DP O(n*budget) could be fine. We'll use dictionary for DP: dp[t] = max score achievable with exactly t tokens. But we need to track which words used. Use bitset or list of indices. Since it's Python, we can store tuple of (score, selected_set) or use DP with reconstruction. Simpler: enumerate all subsets for n<=20, else DP.\n\nBecause this is a coding challenge, we can implement knapsack with DP on number of words, storing best score and the last added word index to reconstruct.\n\nLet's design:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    wt = case[\"word_token_indices\"]\n    ws = case[\"word_scores\"]\n    fw = case[\"forced_words\"]\n    budget = case[\"token_budget\"]\n    n = len(wt)\n\n    # compute word costs\n    costs = [len(indices) for indices in wt]\n    # incorporate forced words: include them, remove from consideration\n    forced_set = set(fw)\n    forced_cost = sum(costs[i] for i in forced_set)\n    forced_score = sum(ws[i] for i in forced_set)\n    remaining_budget = budget - forced_cost\n    if remaining_budget < 0:\n        # cannot include all forced words; maybe return empty? But spec assumes valid.\n        # We'll return empty selected_words and retained_token_indices = list(forced tokens?) But better to raise? We'll return forced words only with budget exceeded? \n        # For safety, we include forced words and cut off budget? Not defined. Let's assume budget enough.\n        pass\n\n    # list of remaining word indices (not forced)\n    remaining_indices = [i for i in range(n) if i not in forced_set]\n  