= len(token_indices) but unique? Assuming disjoint.\n    tokens_used = len(token_indices)  # if tokens are unique across words\n    # If tokens could overlap, we should take unique set: set(token_indices) but then tokens_used = len(set). However, overlap might cause token budget undercounting? The problem is undefined. In typical knapsack, cost is word length, not unique tokens. If tokens overlap, then selecting two words sharing a token might double count token budget? But token budget is number of tokens, not unique tokens. Actually \"token_budget\" likely refers to number of tokens available, and each token is a position. If two words share a token, they still consume that token? That would be weird. So assume disjoint.\n    # We'll trust that input has disjoint token indices per word.\n\n    result = {\n        \"retained_token_indices\": token_indices,\n        \"selected_words\": all_selected,\n        \"tokens_used\": tokens_used\n    }\n    return result\n\nBut need to ensure we obey the output schema exactly: keys as listed. The schema says retained_token_indices is array of integers, selected_words array of integers, tokens_used integer. So our output is fine.\n\nEdge cases: no remaining words => selected_remaining empty. Good.\n\nWe must return a JSON-serializable object. Python dict will be serialized by json.dumps eventually when the evaluator runs. But the function returns object, not JSON string.\n\nImplementation code as string inside JSON envelope.\n\nWe need to write the full code. Use only standard library: functools for lru_cache.\n\nWe need to handle potential recursion depth if many words? But lru_cache works.\n\nWe could implement DP iteratively to avoid recursion limits. For safety, we'll do iterative DP with 2D list using -1 sentinel for unreachable. Since budget could be up to maybe 1000? But number of words might be up to 100? 2D of 101 x 1001 = 101k, fine. But we don't know constraints. However, recursion depth limited to number of words (maybe 100). Python recursion limit default 1000, so safe. We'll use recursion.\n\nAlternative: use DP array of size (remaining_budget+1) and track best score and used set via list of tuples. But reconstruction easier with recursion.\n\nLet's write the function.\n\nWe also need to be careful: case may have _case_nonce but ignore. Use only fields described: word_token_indices, word_scores, forced_words, token_budget.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    import functools\n    wt = case[\"word_token_indices\"]\n    ws = case[\"word_scores\"]\n    fw = case[\"forced_words\"]\n    budget = case[\"token_budget\"]\n    n = len(wt)\n    costs = [len(ind) for ind in wt]\n    forced_set = set(fw)\n    forced_cost = sum(costs[i] for i in forced_set)\n    forced_score = sum(ws[i] for i in forced_set)\n    remaining_budget = budget - forced_cost\n    if remaining_budget < 0:\n        # Cannot fit forced words; return just forced words? Or empty? We'll return forced words only and tokens_used = forced_cost (exceeds budget, but maybe still output)\n        # But problem may guarantee feasibility. For robustness, we'll handle by returning forced words and ignoring budget overrun. Adjust tokens_used to budget? No, we'll keep forced cost.\n        # To be safe, set remaining_budget = 0 and force include forced words? But that would exceed budget.\n        # We'll just include forced words and set tokens_used = forced_cost; selected_words = forced set; retained_token_indices = union of forced tokens.\n        # This might violate budget but better than crash.\n        # Actually, we should not change budget; we just cannot select additional words.\n        remaining_budget = 0\n    remaining_indices = [i for i in range(n) if i not in forced_set]\n    m = len(remaining_indices)\n    @functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n    def dp(idx, cap):\n        if idx == m:\n            return (0, ())\n        i = remaining_indices[idx]\n        cost = costs[i]\n        # skip\n        best_score, best_set = dp(idx+1, cap)\n        # take\n        if cost <= cap:\n            score_take, set_take = dp(idx+1, cap - cost)\n            score_take += ws[i]\n            if score_take > best_score:\n                best_score, best_set = score_take, (i,) + set_take\n        return (best_score, best_set)\n    best_score, selected_remaining = dp(0, remaining_budget)\n    selected_remaining = list(selected_remaining)\n    all_selected = sorted(forced_set.union(selected_remaining))\n    token_indices = []\n    for i in all_selected:\n        token_indices.extend(wt[i])\n    token_indices.sort()\n    tokens_used = len(token_indices)  # assumes disjoint\n    return {\n        \"retained_token_indices\": token_indices,\n        \"selected_words\": all_selected,\n        \"tokens_used\": tokens_used\n    }\n\nNow, we need to wrap in implementation key. The output must be a JSON object with \"implementation\" containing the Python source string. So we need to produce a JSON string that includes the code with proper escaping. We'll write the code as a string literal within JSON.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import functools\\n    wt = case[\\\"word_token_indices\\\"]\\n    ws = case[\\\"word_scores\\\"]\\n    fw = case[\\\"forced_words\\\"]\\n    budget = case[\\\"token_budget\\\"]\\n    n = len(wt)\\n    costs = [len(ind) for ind in wt]\\n    forced_set = set(fw)\\n    forced_cost = sum(costs[i] for i in forced_set)\\n    forced_score = sum(ws[i] for i in forced_set)\\n    remaining_budget = budget - forced_cost\\n    if remaining_budget < 0:\\n        remaining_budget = 0\\n    remaining_indices = [i for i in range(n) if i not in forced_set]\\n    m = len(remaining_indices)\\n    @functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)\\n    def dp(idx, cap):\\n        if idx == m:\\n            return (0, ())\\n        i = remaining_indices[idx]\\n        cost = costs[i]\\n        best_score, best_set = dp(idx+1, cap)\\n        if cost <= cap:\\